  "summary": "Gate of Thunder is Red Company's side-scrolling shooter, released for PC Engine's Super CD-ROM² in Japan on 21 February 1992.",
  "body": [
    "Gate of Thunder is Red Company's side-scrolling shooter, released for PC Engine's Super CD-ROM² in Japan on 21 February 1992. Hawk pilots the Hunting Dog against an armada seeking the Starlight energy source, with Esty's Wild Cat providing support. Seven stages carry the ship through military opposition and large bosses. Its dense scenery and recorded rock music give the journey considerable force, while the player's immediate task remains clear: find a safe line through the fire and bring the next enemy into range.",
    "The Hunting Dog collects weapons and supporting firepower as the screen advances. Threats arrive from different directions, requiring attention to the space behind the ship as well as the formation ahead. Large enemies and stage obstacles break up that movement before each boss encounter. The ship's weapons provide ways to answer those changing arrangements, but positioning determines how safely an advantage can be used. Four difficulty settings alter the challenge, giving the same sequence of seven stages different demands as the player becomes more familiar with its patterns.",
    "The team within Red Company included former Technosoft staff who had worked on Thunder Force, and later formed CAProduction. Nick Wood of Sound Design composed the score. Increased memory available through Super CD-ROM² supported the detailed presentation. In North America, Gate of Thunder accompanied the TurboDuo on a disc that also included Bonk's Adventure, Bonk's Revenge and Bomberman. That bundle made the shooter a prominent demonstration of the new hardware, with its fast action and recorded music immediately illustrating what the disc-based system could deliver."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A fighter crossing seven battles",
    "Firepower that can face behind",
    "A showcase for the Super CD"
  ],
